      "Notes": "An Arduino Mega 2560 with the openSciLab Arduino firmware at the C64 expansion port, wired after Ben Eater's 6502 monitor: the address bus on the even pins 22-52 (A0 on 22 ... A15 on 52), the data bus on the odd pins 31-45 (D0 on 31 ... D7 on 45), the clock Phi2 (E) on D2 (captured, too: a timing capture shows it and decodes on it), R/W (5) on D3 and /RESET (C) on D53. The other lines of the port are not captured: address, data and R/W are what a cycle needs for the disassembly. GND to pins A and Z. The C64 and the Mega both run at 5 V; the pins stay inputs (never set them as outputs while they are on the bus). State capture: a frame per falling edge of Phi2 (D2 is the interrupt INT4), the buses Address and Data show each cycle as Ben Eater's monitor prints it. The Mega takes an edge in its interrupt and sends about 1,000 frames a second over USB (11 bytes each, its ring holds 372): that suits a slowed or single-stepped 6502 as on Ben Eater's breadboard computer; a C64 runs Phi2 at about 1 MHz, far too fast - most of its cycles are lost and counted as lost edges. The C64 bus decoder is set up with Phi2 on the clock: every state is a Phi2 edge, the decoder shows the bus cycles and disassembles them. All pins: R/W D3, Φ2 D2, /RESET D53, A0 D22, A1 D24, A2 D26, A3 D28, A4 D30, A5 D32, A6 D34, A7 D36, A8 D38, A9 D40, A10 D42, A11 D44, A12 D46, A13 D48, A14 D50, A15 D52, D0 D31, D1 D33, D2 D35, D3 D37, D4 D39, D5 D41, D6 D43, D7 D45."
